86
00:08:45,328 --> 00:08:49,213
So we have the delegated
access, where it acts on behalf

87
00:08:49,830 --> 00:08:51,950
of a user, a signed-in user.

88
00:08:52,259 --> 00:08:58,148
So the access of this agent is now
subjected based on the user's access,

89
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:03,736
the granted scope of this user, and
also resource checks of this user.

90
00:09:04,383 --> 00:09:09,375
So the access of that agent
is limited to the user scope,

91
00:09:09,478 --> 00:09:11,222
the access, and all of that.

92
00:09:12,111 --> 00:09:20,196
So if that user has permissions
to a broad, range of data sources,

93
00:09:20,695 --> 00:09:25,550
sensitive documents, so also that
agent has that access as well.

94
00:09:26,771 --> 00:09:31,347
The next is the emerging
one, which is the digital…

95
00:09:31,379 --> 00:09:33,273
I like to call it a digital worker.

96
00:09:33,634 --> 00:09:37,639
An agent operating on his own
account, having his own user account.

97
00:09:38,801 --> 00:09:43,154
The agent has his own email, just like an
employee, you will have your own email.

98
00:09:43,689 --> 00:09:47,868
The agent can work with collaboration
too, so has his own teams.

99
00:09:48,526 --> 00:09:52,743
So it is operating on
his own identity as well.

100
00:09:53,959 --> 00:10:00,013
Then the other one is the app-only
access, so where it uses an application

101
00:10:00,017 --> 00:10:04,054
identity, often for background operations.

102
00:10:05,612 --> 00:10:10,748
So if permissions are broad
for all of these, so also the

103
00:10:10,748 --> 00:10:12,986
exposure can also be broad, too.

104
00:10:13,785 --> 00:10:19,915
And if such identity are compromised,
then what could be the impact as well?

105
00:10:20,795 --> 00:10:26,159
So this, where we have, you know,
whose keys is your a-agent holding?

106
00:10:26,486 --> 00:10:36,013
So as a design engineer or a developer,
while building your agent, you have

107
00:10:36,013 --> 00:10:41,485
to think about on whose permission
is this agen-i-agent going to run?

108
00:10:41,546 --> 00:10:45,289
On whose identity is it going to run on?

109
00:10:45,620 --> 00:10:50,365
So first, is it, does it have an identity?

110
00:10:51,365 --> 00:10:54,994
What can that identity actually do?

111
00:10:55,037 --> 00:11:00,494
So the question is not only about
if it has an identity, but also

112
00:11:00,945 --> 00:11:04,463
what can that identity actually do?

113
00:11:05,463 --> 00:11:08,566
This takes me now to the
Model Context Protocol.

114
00:11:09,945 --> 00:11:12,978
The MCP is a protocol,
not a trust decision.

115
00:11:13,642 --> 00:11:18,512
So it basically standardizes
your, your integration.

116
00:11:18,595 --> 00:11:22,887
So it gives agents a standard
way to discover and use tools.

117
00:11:23,592 --> 00:11:29,851
So think of our agent right now, our
customer support agent connecting

118
00:11:29,852 --> 00:11:32,273
to a support ticket MCP server

119
00:11:33,273 --> 00:11:35,769
which then calls an enterprise API.

120
00:11:36,555 --> 00:11:37,769
Convenient, right?

121
00:11:37,881 --> 00:11:38,539
Fast?

122
00:11:39,092 --> 00:11:39,915
Absolutely.

123
00:11:40,579 --> 00:11:42,680
But is it automatically trusted?

124
00:11:44,412 --> 00:11:47,447
Should that MCP server
be automatically trusted?

125
00:11:47,981 --> 00:11:48,410
No.

126
00:11:49,868 --> 00:11:52,242
Look at the search ticket description.

127
00:11:53,028 --> 00:11:58,365
It quietly asks the model to
