128
00:11:59,143 --> 00:12:01,737
That's tool description poisoning.

129
00:12:03,010 --> 00:12:08,550
Tool metadata can influence model
deci- decisions, even though

130
00:12:08,897 --> 00:12:13,256
it doesn't necessarily have the
priority of system instructions.

131
00:12:14,032 --> 00:12:18,685
So it's good to always review the
servers, the tool definition, the tool

132
00:12:18,685 --> 00:12:25,809
descriptions, and restrict discovery,
and also validate requests and enforce

133
00:12:25,836 --> 00:12:33,255
permissions at execution That brings me
to another more important question then.

134
00:12:33,797 --> 00:12:36,238
Can it call the tool?

135
00:12:37,238 --> 00:12:42,481
Your agent can call the tools it has
access, it has dis-- it can discover tools

136
00:12:43,481 --> 00:12:44,577
But should it?

137
00:12:45,577 --> 00:12:50,556
Capability doesn't
always mean authorization

138
00:12:51,556 --> 00:12:57,187
Here, the model proposes
deleting a customer ID

139
00:12:58,187 --> 00:13:01,681
The application checks the
policy before executing it.

140
00:13:02,576 --> 00:13:09,699
So if the deletion requires an
approver and none exists, the

141
00:13:09,728 --> 00:13:15,016
call is denied and logged as well
for telemetry and auditability.

142
00:13:16,635 --> 00:13:22,446
Notice the distinction Writing an
instruction like "Please don't delete

143
00:13:22,486 --> 00:13:29,754
data" in your instruction while you're
defining the scope of your agent is just a

144
00:13:29,768 --> 00:13:34,292
suggestion and not an authorization check

145
00:13:35,292 --> 00:13:40,515
An independent authorization check is
a boundary, so you need to have that

146
00:13:40,531 --> 00:13:46,009
policy check, a boundary that governs
the scope or access of this agent.

147
00:13:46,545 --> 00:13:53,057
And this matters specifically for
agents that do critical activities

148
00:13:54,018 --> 00:13:59,139
from deleting records, external
sharing, or performing transactions.

149
00:14:00,873 --> 00:14:07,557
We can let models propose actions without
letting them grant themselves permissions.

150
00:14:09,025 --> 00:14:14,326
Let's connect the document, the model,
and the tool into one at- attack path

151
00:14:15,326 --> 00:14:17,533
First, we have entry.

152
00:14:17,676 --> 00:14:22,414
The agent retrieves that
poisoned refund policy.

153
00:14:23,687 --> 00:14:25,284
And the second is prompt injection.

154
00:14:26,248 --> 00:14:29,999
It mistakes the document text
for an instruction and tries

155
00:14:30,001 --> 00:14:32,182
to export customers' cases.

156
00:14:32,743 --> 00:14:34,255
And third, the boundary.

157
00:14:35,761 --> 00:14:42,314
The tool or API rejects that unauthorized
or malicious export and records that

158
00:14:42,350 --> 00:14:50,369
attempt for visibility Retrieval checks
might reduce the chance of injection,

159
00:14:51,871 --> 00:14:58,625
but authorization limits what happens
if those retrieval checks miss it.

160
00:14:59,317 --> 00:15:04,395
And telemetry tells us what was
attempted, the activities, the kind

161
00:15:04,396 --> 00:15:08,779
of files that was-- that contains the
malicious prompts and all of that.

162
00:15:09,697 --> 00:15:14,944
So here you can see that the model
was fooled or the model was tricked,

163
00:15:15,374 --> 00:15:20,657
but the system held based on the
boundary that was being implemented.

164
00:15:21,508 --> 00:15:23,332
So that's the design goal.

165
00:15:23,950 --> 00:15:28,136
Not pretending that your model
can never be manipulated, but

166
00:15:28,202 --> 00:15:30,248
containing the consequences.

167
00:15:30,684 --> 00:15:34,616
So where do we place these
controls along the path?

168
00:15:35,616 --> 00:15:37,989
Guardrails don't belong
in the prompt alone.

169
00:15:38,914 --> 00:15:43,831
So they cut across several intervention
points from your inputs, your tools,

170
00:15:43,833 --> 00:15:45,547
your actions, and your out-outputs.

171
00:15:46,579 --> 00:15:52,304
We can validate inputs, inspect
retrieved context, apply

172
00:15:52,331 --> 00:15:57,480
model-level checks, validate tool
arguments, and even authorize

173
00:15:57,539 --> 00:16:00,660
actions and check outputs as well.

174
00:16:02,655 --> 00:16:07,470
In our example, the prompt or the
request made by the customer was ins-

175
00:16:07,772 --> 00:16:15,494
innocent, was a legitimate prompt, but
the knowledge base contained a malicious

176
00:16:15,552 --> 00:16:20,261
instruction hidden in the policy document.

177
00:16:21,908 --> 00:16:27,052
A filter might have-- might flag it,
but that same filter might also miss it.

178
00:16:27,984 --> 00:16:32,381
But a properly enforced export
permission can still block

179
00:16:32,387 --> 00:16:33,906
the action from going through.

180
00:16:34,338 --> 00:16:41,507
So as a dis-- engineer or a developer,
some controls you implement could

181
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:49,485
detect or reduce the risk or others
enforce explicit rules like blocking

182
00:16:50,451 --> 00:16:52,695
that action from being executed.

183
00:16:54,132 --> 00:17:00,919
The important question is where
each control sits, what it checks,

184
00:17:01,187 --> 00:17:04,356
and what happens when it says no

185
00:17:05,355 --> 00:17:07,024
This takes me to the gateway.

186
00:17:07,797 --> 00:17:12,324
So the gateway centralizes
the model traffic.

187
00:17:12,428 --> 00:17:18,180
An AI gateway can be shared, can be a
shared front door for model calls, for

188
00:17:18,180 --> 00:17:25,480
your IP filtering, for model routing, for
authentication, and also your telemetry

189
00:17:26,119 --> 00:17:33,532
and supported policies instead of every
team managing model endpoint differently.

190
00:17:33,797 --> 00:17:40,135
So the platform team, it offers them
a reusable service, but don't confuse

191
00:17:40,814 --> 00:17:43,619
